# /etc/willow-mcp/manifest-grant.env — root:willow-operator 0640
# Sealed pairs 1fdfbdf3 (system unit under the trust owner) and 1bd6fd29
# (setup is the installer's; operations are Willow's).
# Only what `willow-mcp manifest-grant apply` reads. No provider keys, no
# store coordinates, nothing from the broker's own env file.
#
# Public-only verifier ring (the net signer's; root-owned, never writable by
# the service uid) — verify_seal checks the sealed pair's ed25519 seal_sig
# against it. Every one of the five verbs on this queue (manifest.grant,
# envelope.revoke, manifest.retire, manifest.create, federation.ratify)
# checks the same ring.
WILLOW_KEYRING=/etc/willow-mcp/verifiers.public.json
# Dispatch 0CB0C85C (amending B291C0C7): WILLOW_PGP_FINGERPRINT is no longer
# set here. It used to be — a second copy of the same value $H/env carried,
# which this unit (uid willow-operator) could never check against the
# source ($H/env is 0600), so the two could silently drift (Loki audit
# A38D41C2, F8). The trust-owner apply half now reads it directly from
# $WILLOW_HOME/constitutional/trust.env — trust-owner-owned, world-readable,
# the SAME file the broker, serve, and the desk all read. install.sh writes
# that file; nothing needs to render a copy into this one.
# FRANK: the apply confirms its citation and inks manifest_granted (or one of
# envelope_revoke_applied / manifest_retire_applied / manifest_create_applied /
# federation_ratify_applied) there. Same database as the broker; the role is
# the service uid's own (peer auth).
WILLOW_PG_DB=
WILLOW_PG_USER=willow-operator
# Hardened posture — the whole point of the uid split.
WILLOW_MCP_STRICT_TRUST_ROOT=1
